  "enhanced_information": "Iron absorption in the intestine is more effective in the form of heme group (option opb), as heme iron is readily absorbed without the need for reduction, leading to higher bioavailability. In contrast, ferric ions (option opa) must be reduced to ferrous ions for absorption, making them less effective. The transferrin transporter (option opc) is involved in iron transport but is not the primary mechanism for intestinal absorption; it primarily functions in iron transport in the bloodstream. Lastly, oxalates (option opd) bind to iron and inhibit its absorption, reducing bioavailability. Therefore, the most effective form of iron absorption is through the heme group."
